(252201, 15976, 1280, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/>The colors here are just lovely, and they represent Spring beautifully. The purple background is very nice, but not so much as to be distracting.  I am curious about what is the bg comprised of, but that''s besides the point. Your depth of field (dof) is good, and sufficient to blur the bg enough to make the subject stand out. A slight improvement would be to have the foreground subject just a little sharper, but it is within the bounds of acceptable in my opinion.<br/>The exposure is good, allowing great visibility of the subject.<br/>The composition is strong, with a good diagonal lead line.<br/>Post processing is fine, with no visible artifacts or noise.  <br/>As for meeting the challenge, well....in my mind''s eye it''s sort of a stretch, and as someone mentioned, a play on words, but it comes in close.  I wouldn''t personally have consdidered this as a "time" picture for my own submission, but in a very broad sense, it is.<br/>Overall, I like this photo.  It''s very pretty and you did a good job capturing it. Keep up the good work! :-)<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Grayce<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!The colors here are just lovely, and they represent Spring beautifully. The purple background is very nice, but not so much as to be distracting.  I am curious about what is the bg comprised of, but that''s besides the point. Your depth of field (dof) is good, and sufficient to blur the bg enough to make the subject stand out. A slight improvement would be to have the foreground subject just a little sharper, but it is within the bounds of acceptable in my opinion.The exposure is good, allowing great visibility of the subject.The composition is strong, with a good diagonal lead line.Post processing is fine, with no visible artifacts or noise.  As for meeting the challenge, well....in my mind''s eye it''s sort of a stretch, and as someone mentioned, a play on words, but it comes in close.  I wouldn''t personally have consdidered this as a "time" picture for my own submission, but in a very broad sense, it is.Overall, I like this photo.  It''s very pretty and you did a good job capturing it. Keep up the good work! :-)Regards, Grayce', '2003-04-01 21:52:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(252252, 15872, 1863, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/>Hi Crabappl3!<br/> <br/>I always loved antiquated time pieces, and darn if this isn''t a good example of one. I like your choice of subject a lot. I also think your technical choices were good. But.. the light was just a tiny bit too harsh in my opinion. <br/>Cropping is an issue with me on this photo as well. As much as I like the tree stump, I also think that it''s distracting from the main subject. I would have (personally) prefered to see you focus more on the uhm sundial, and crop it more around that. <br/>I like the deail you have managed to capture. Well done.<br/>-Annida</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club!Hi Crabappl3! I always loved antiquated time pieces, and darn if this isn''t a good example of one. I like your choice of subject a lot. I also think your technical choices were good. But.. the light was just a tiny bit too harsh in my opinion. Cropping is an issue with me on this photo as well. As much as I like the tree stump, I also think that it''s distracting from the main subject. I would have (personally) prefered to see you focus more on the uhm sundial, and crop it more around that. I like the deail you have managed to capture. Well done.-Annida', '2003-04-01 22:58:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(252289, 15956, 4877, '<td valign="top">I was trying to come up with an original idea for this photography challenge when I stumbled across this scene.  I was waking through The Rocks in Sydney and found this store which takes has costumes and allows you to dress up and have antique photos taken on these cameras in my photo.  After asking nicely, the store attendant allowed me to take some photos.  I got the concept right the first go, but my composition wasnt precisely what I wanted, and there was string and a few other things on the ground.  So I went back later in the week for a re-shoot.  This is what I came up with.  There was little I could do about re-arranging the furtniture, and I could only remove the most obvious "debris".  But it turned out quite well all things considered.</td>', 'I was trying to come up with an original idea for this photography challenge when I stumbled across this scene.  I was waking through The Rocks in Sydney and found this store which takes has costumes and allows you to dress up and have antique photos taken on these cameras in my photo.  After asking nicely, the store attendant allowed me to take some photos.  I got the concept right the first go, but my composition wasnt precisely what I wanted, and there was string and a few other things on the ground.  So I went back later in the week for a re-shoot.  This is what I came up with.  There was little I could do about re-arranging the furtniture, and I could only remove the most obvious "debris".  But it turned out quite well all things considered.', '2003-04-01 23:18:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(252351, 15873, 772, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club :)<br/> <br/>Hi Bill...<br/> <br/>This is an interesting abstract for sure... I also like the choice of composition on this photo.  Orienting your subject and reflection on the right side of the frame seems to work well overall.  One thing you may have tried here would have been to do a nice diagonal orientation as well.  Diagonal orientations on some subjects seem to create a bit of eye interest that is not your usual path.  <br/> <br/>Your choice of black and white seems to work nicely here too, but when you are dealing with a reflective surface, you can also do some really intriguing work with color by allowing color patterns to reflect on your surface...<br/> <br/>Keep up the good work :)<br/> <br/>John Setzler<br/></td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club :) Hi Bill... This is an interesting abstract for sure... I also like the choice of composition on this photo.  Orienting your subject and reflection on the right side of the frame seems to work well overall.  One thing you may have tried here would have been to do a nice diagonal orientation as well.  Diagonal orientations on some subjects seem to create a bit of eye interest that is not your usual path.   Your choice of black and white seems to work nicely here too, but when you are dealing with a reflective surface, you can also do some really intriguing work with color by allowing color patterns to reflect on your surface... Keep up the good work :) John Setzler', '2003-04-01 23:39:54.000000', NULL, 0),
(252379, 15891, 6110, '<td valign="top">Thanks everyone for all the nice comments.<br/> <br/>Achiral:  The shot was actually taken mid-day in a sunlight room.  I partially wrapped the clock and camera with black foam to get the exposure I wanted, and to control reflections.<a href="//www.pbase.com/image/14998554/original.jpg" rel="nofollow" target="_blank">Here''s what the setup looks like.</a> <br/> <br/>The hardest part of this shot -- aside from the dust -- was focus. Each time I modified the composition, I''d have to wait for the second hand to return to xx:35, and then had to work fast. :-)<br/> <br/>e301 -- The clock is the sort that advances to the next second very quickly, then also stops quickly -- that''s why you don''t really see any motion blur between the seconds.  <br/> <br/>Swash -- Even though the second hand stops for almost a full second, I still needed to experiment with the angle of the camera so the photo would show enough of a side view.  I had a few shots taken from a more vertical angle, and the second hand is hardly visible -- it''s too thin.</td>', 'Thanks everyone for all the nice comments. Achiral:  The shot was actually taken mid-day in a sunlight room.  I partially wrapped the clock and camera with black foam to get the exposure I wanted, and to control reflections.Here''s what the setup looks like.  The hardest part of this shot -- aside from the dust -- was focus. Each time I modified the composition, I''d have to wait for the second hand to return to xx:35, and then had to work fast. :-) e301 -- The clock is the sort that advances to the next second very quickly, then also stops quickly -- that''s why you don''t really see any motion blur between the seconds.   Swash -- Even though the second hand stops for almost a full second, I still needed to experiment with the angle of the camera so the photo would show enough of a side view.  I had a few shots taken from a more vertical angle, and the second hand is hardly visible -- it''s too thin.', '2003-04-01 23:56:16.000000', NULL, 0),
(252394, 15931, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC time for Geocide </b> <br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge- Yes<br/>Composition- Nicely done<br/>Background- n/a here<br/>My Opinion- This is a shot I''d like to try....I like that it''s dark except for the lit buttons. Neat looking. I thought it would do better then it did. It was a huge challenge and maybe it just got lost in all the others. Doesn''t make it a bad shot......well done.<br/></td>', 'CC time for Geocide   Fits The Challenge- YesComposition- Nicely doneBackground- n/a hereMy Opinion- This is a shot I''d like to try....I like that it''s dark except for the lit buttons. Neat looking. I thought it would do better then it did. It was a huge challenge and maybe it just got lost in all the others. Doesn''t make it a bad shot......well done.', '2003-04-02 00:04:15.000000', NULL, 0),
(252414, 15401, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC for you psychephylax </b> <br/> <br/>I will make this short since I already did a mini CC in the forums yesterday.<br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes. <br/>Composition-Okay but the pole and trash distracted from the shot as a whole. <br/>My Opinion- I guess that pole/trash are the main things. I would of liked more of the pond/lake in the photo. The man is good, I like the feel of the shot, alone, lost, sad. <br/>:)<br/> <br/></td>', 'CC for you psychephylax   I will make this short since I already did a mini CC in the forums yesterday.Fits The Challenge-Yes. Composition-Okay but the pole and trash distracted from the shot as a whole. My Opinion- I guess that pole/trash are the main things. I would of liked more of the pond/lake in the photo. The man is good, I like the feel of the shot, alone, lost, sad. :) ', '2003-04-02 00:15:30.000000', NULL, 0),
