(253116, 15920, 5212, '<td valign="top">Critique Club:<br/> <br/>Meeting the challenge: Well done, although I would have some difficulty seeing this as a time picture if not for the challenge and title (a flaw with my entry, too) I actually wanted to do something like this and couldn''t find the right opportunity to do so.<br/> <br/>Composition: Despite comments below, I (for the most part) like the composition of the shot, straight lines and angles vs. curves, lighting and contrast used for emphasis as well as the dark upper border vs the light stone texture.  There is a little something on the left edge that I don''t understand and might have been better if more were showing or if it was cropped out.  The shadow at the bottom right also is a little confusing to me.<br/> <br/>Technical Quality: The photo is clear and no post-processing problems noted.  The lighting might be harsh for another photo, but works well to illustrate the meaning of the shot for the most part.<br/> <br/>Creativity: One of the few shots without a plain old clock or watch, well done.  As you noted above, it did lack some wow factor and that probably did affect your score.  AT the same time, it was different than any of the other entries that I remember which is a plus.<br/> <br/>Overall: A fairly nice shot for this challenge, don''t think I would have considered it below a 5, though.  Would have at least given it a five and maybe six.<br/> <br/>Good luck, I think your shots are improving.  Thanks for letting me critique this shot!!  Kandice<br/> <br/></td>', 'Critique Club: Meeting the challenge: Well done, although I would have some difficulty seeing this as a time picture if not for the challenge and title (a flaw with my entry, too) I actually wanted to do something like this and couldn''t find the right opportunity to do so. Composition: Despite comments below, I (for the most part) like the composition of the shot, straight lines and angles vs. curves, lighting and contrast used for emphasis as well as the dark upper border vs the light stone texture.  There is a little something on the left edge that I don''t understand and might have been better if more were showing or if it was cropped out.  The shadow at the bottom right also is a little confusing to me. Technical Quality: The photo is clear and no post-processing problems noted.  The lighting might be harsh for another photo, but works well to illustrate the meaning of the shot for the most part. Creativity: One of the few shots without a plain old clock or watch, well done.  As you noted above, it did lack some wow factor and that probably did affect your score.  AT the same time, it was different than any of the other entries that I remember which is a plus. Overall: A fairly nice shot for this challenge, don''t think I would have considered it below a 5, though.  Would have at least given it a five and maybe six. Good luck, I think your shots are improving.  Thanks for letting me critique this shot!!  Kandice ', '2003-04-02 19:24:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(253152, 15866, 6458, '<td valign="top">Greets from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Initial Impression:<br/>Interesting composition, colors seem a bit off, focus seems soft.<br/> <br/>Challenge:<br/>Meets the challenge fine<br/> <br/>Composition/Creativity:<br/>This is the strongest point of this photo in my opinion. The angle that you shot this at is very interesting... Diagonals tend to make pictures seem more dynamic and it does just that in this picture. My only problem is the tree.. It clutters the view. I would prefer to just see the clock and the clouds! I think it would have give the photo a cleaner look and much more visual impact!<br/> <br/>Technical/Post processing: <br/>The exposure is good in my opinion. Some may say the sky is a bit underexposed, but that just serves to frame the clock, good job. For some reason this photo feels fuzzy to me, I think an unsharpen-mask would have sharpened things up and removed that minor irritation. <br/> <br/>Overall: <br/>An interesting photo, good eye! If it was clean and simple, it would have had more of the "wow" factor..  Keep up the good work!<br/> <br/>-Matt</td>', 'Greets from the Critique Club! Initial Impression:Interesting composition, colors seem a bit off, focus seems soft. Challenge:Meets the challenge fine Composition/Creativity:This is the strongest point of this photo in my opinion. The angle that you shot this at is very interesting... Diagonals tend to make pictures seem more dynamic and it does just that in this picture. My only problem is the tree.. It clutters the view. I would prefer to just see the clock and the clouds! I think it would have give the photo a cleaner look and much more visual impact! Technical/Post processing: The exposure is good in my opinion. Some may say the sky is a bit underexposed, but that just serves to frame the clock, good job. For some reason this photo feels fuzzy to me, I think an unsharpen-mask would have sharpened things up and removed that minor irritation.  Overall: An interesting photo, good eye! If it was clean and simple, it would have had more of the "wow" factor..  Keep up the good work! -Matt', '2003-04-02 20:23:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(253226, 15883, 327, '<td valign="top"><b>CC time for you PHOTOCHlX </b> <br/> <br/>Fits The Challenge-Yes.....showing spring time, and of course the title tells us that also. <br/>Composition-Okay<br/>Background-Pretty good, maybe sitting on a picnic table or wooden bench. Neat looking aged wood. <br/>My Opinion-Photoshicx this shot did work out well. The reason is the flowers are out of focus. (OOF) There is really only one full flower seen in the shot and that is the one that''s dead center. Because it''s in the center it should be in focus. <br/>Yaaaaahee, <b>no big deal</b>, your set up was okay the yellow is good and it was learning experience. Enter the next one and grab yer self a ribbon!!!!! See ya! Good luck!<br/></td>', 'CC time for you PHOTOCHlX   Fits The Challenge-Yes.....showing spring time, and of course the title tells us that also. Composition-OkayBackground-Pretty good, maybe sitting on a picnic table or wooden bench. Neat looking aged wood. My Opinion-Photoshicx this shot did work out well. The reason is the flowers are out of focus. (OOF) There is really only one full flower seen in the shot and that is the one that''s dead center. Because it''s in the center it should be in focus. Yaaaaahee, no big deal, your set up was okay the yellow is good and it was learning experience. Enter the next one and grab yer self a ribbon!!!!! See ya! Good luck!', '2003-04-02 22:06:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(253332, 15727, 1863, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/>Hello crzystarbuckzgrl!<br/>Wow, what a name you have, so hard to type up *laughs*<br/> <br/>Let''s get down to business :)<br/> <br/>The composition in this photo is just great. Very nicely balanced.. The bush on the left draws the eye to the clock up in the right hand, which works nicely. <br/>The sepia tone seems to fit nicely. Did you try plain black and white? I''m not convinced... I"m not sure what it is. Maybe it''s the border which doesn''t fit with the way the photo is set up. You want people to believe this is an oldish photograph, and at the same time you add a stark black border, which just doesn''t fit.<br/>The lighting seems a little bit harsh, and I think I would have prefered to see more into the clock, although then you might lose the composition you put into it. Ah, I see why you lost detail in the clock. your aperture (the f rating) was a bit too shallow. Did you try around an F6-8? you would have gotten more depth, and you would have had the clock more in focus.<br/>Good luck in the next challenges!<br/>-Annida</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club!Hello crzystarbuckzgrl!Wow, what a name you have, so hard to type up *laughs* Let''s get down to business :) The composition in this photo is just great. Very nicely balanced.. The bush on the left draws the eye to the clock up in the right hand, which works nicely. The sepia tone seems to fit nicely. Did you try plain black and white? I''m not convinced... I"m not sure what it is. Maybe it''s the border which doesn''t fit with the way the photo is set up. You want people to believe this is an oldish photograph, and at the same time you add a stark black border, which just doesn''t fit.The lighting seems a little bit harsh, and I think I would have prefered to see more into the clock, although then you might lose the composition you put into it. Ah, I see why you lost detail in the clock. your aperture (the f rating) was a bit too shallow. Did you try around an F6-8? you would have gotten more depth, and you would have had the clock more in focus.Good luck in the next challenges!-Annida', '2003-04-03 00:50:33.000000', NULL, 0),
(253360, 15650, 5947, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by kiwiness:</b></div><hr/><i>Wonderful use of natural lighting and shadows. The resulting image is perfect - 10.</i></td></tr></table> Thank you so much! I really apprieciate the comment!</td>', 'Originally posted by kiwiness:Wonderful use of natural lighting and shadows. The resulting image is perfect - 10. Thank you so much! I really apprieciate the comment!', '2003-04-03 01:37:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(253361, 15838, 6328, '<td valign="top">Time is over<br/>By pikyto<br/>Critique by Silver Fox<br/> <br/>First of all Paulo, I would like to ask you to please start filling in the information on aperture and shutter information and give us some information on the Photographers comments, i.e. how you took the photo, etc. enhancement, tone, etc.  <br/> <br/>The first thing I notice about your photograph of the watch is that there were no hands on it, and then the great detail that you were able to obtain by your macro focusing of the watch and hand, but the focus seems more so on the hand and the watch seems just a bit out of focus.  <br/> <br/>Your composition is good.<br/> <br/>The lighting had to be near perfect in order to obtain the focus that you obtained, so bravo for that also, although you can see a bit of glare on the right hand side of the watch  but are we all perfect?<br/> <br/>You obtained good coloring and image in the photo.  (Did you sepia tone it slightly?)   I especially like the background colour that you chose for this shot.<br/> <br/> Nice details on the finger and thumb of the man holding the watch.<br/> <br/>You had an excellent idea for the Challenge and you show imagination and originality in your work.<br/> <br/>I notice from your past Challenges that youre finishing in the averages of 5.4s, so all I can say is to keep practicing and every day that you can and enter the Challenges as often as you can and you will be able to sit back and watch you scores slowly climb higher and higher.<br/>Regards,<br/> <br/>Ed<br/></td>', 'Time is overBy pikytoCritique by Silver Fox First of all Paulo, I would like to ask you to please start filling in the information on aperture and shutter information and give us some information on the Photographers comments, i.e. how you took the photo, etc. enhancement, tone, etc.   The first thing I notice about your photograph of the watch is that there were no hands on it, and then the great detail that you were able to obtain by your macro focusing of the watch and hand, but the focus seems more so on the hand and the watch seems just a bit out of focus.   Your composition is good. The lighting had to be near perfect in order to obtain the focus that you obtained, so bravo for that also, although you can see a bit of glare on the right hand side of the watch  but are we all perfect? You obtained good coloring and image in the photo.  (Did you sepia tone it slightly?)   I especially like the background colour that you chose for this shot.  Nice details on the finger and thumb of the man holding the watch. You had an excellent idea for the Challenge and you show imagination and originality in your work. I notice from your past Challenges that youre finishing in the averages of 5.4s, so all I can say is to keep practicing and every day that you can and enter the Challenges as often as you can and you will be able to sit back and watch you scores slowly climb higher and higher.Regards, Ed', '2003-04-03 01:38:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(253363, 15650, 5947, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by jmsetzler:</b></div><hr/><i>beautiful lighting and composition... great shot :) = 10 - setzler</i></td></tr></table>Thank you so much! I have only participated in two challenges but getting a 10 really makes me feel good! Thank you!</td>', 'Originally posted by jmsetzler:beautiful lighting and composition... great shot :) = 10 - setzlerThank you so much! I have only participated in two challenges but getting a 10 really makes me feel good! Thank you!', '2003-04-03 01:39:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(253366, 15650, 5947, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by Shiiizzzam:</b></div><hr/><i>Ohhhhh this is my fav so far ! The tone and light just sets the mood. The awesome shaker ect are right on ! This is my first 10 ! What a great print this will make !!!!!</i></td></tr></table>Thank you! I really apprieciate that! This was really an easy shot but I''m really glad that you liked it!</td>', 'Originally posted by Shiiizzzam:Ohhhhh this is my fav so far ! The tone and light just sets the mood. The awesome shaker ect are right on ! This is my first 10 ! What a great print this will make !!!!!Thank you! I really apprieciate that! This was really an easy shot but I''m really glad that you liked it!', '2003-04-03 01:40:58.000000', NULL, 0),
